        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-250397",
        ":question/problem": "Rewrite the following expression as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3 & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
